{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-67330",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the first term is $f(1)=-11$ and the common difference is $d=3.$ So, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-11+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =-11+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=3n-14.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
